1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Fast, furious and deadly, modern warfare is the most intensely demanding activity ever undertaken by mankind.

2
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
But to wage war you must first practice it.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Next on Modern Marvels, War Games.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
With dozens of potential hotspots scattered around the globe,

5
00:00:44,620 --> 00:00:48,240
today's Army must be ready to deploy virtually anywhere at a moment's notice.

6
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:51,840
To achieve this extremely high level of combat readiness,

7
00:00:52,440 --> 00:00:56,040
equipment and personnel must be maintained at peak performance at all times.

8
00:00:56,620 --> 00:01:02,260
The challenge for a commander is to find a way to reach and maintain this razor's edge of preparation.

9
00:01:03,340 --> 00:01:07,140
The answer is rigorous force-on-force combat exercises called war games.

10
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:12,360
The place where the most dynamically realistic war games ever devised are staged

11
00:01:12,361 --> 00:01:16,360
is a modern marvel called the U.S. Army National Training Center.

12
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:20,000
Though the soldiers here may be engaging in war gaming,

13
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:22,740
the goal of their deployment is deadly serious.

14
00:01:22,741 --> 00:01:26,520
as one veteran army general said after the Persian Gulf War.

15
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:30,700
We died at Fort Irwin so we could survive a desert storm.

16
00:01:34,820 --> 00:01:37,440
Home to the U.S. Army's National Training Center,

17
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:41,800
Fort Irwin, California is one of America's preeminent combat training facilities.

18
00:01:42,740 --> 00:01:45,560
The base is situated in the heart of the Mojave Desert

19
00:01:45,561 --> 00:01:48,780
on over 640,000 acres near Death Valley,

20
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:52,660
a sprawling, parched expanse as large as the state of Rhode Island.

21
00:01:52,740 --> 00:02:04,085
Each year, approximately ten army rotations, consisting of roughly 5,000 men and women at a time, descend upon this no-man's land to do battle under the critical eye

22
00:02:04,086 --> 00:02:05,460
of their commanders.

23
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:16,582
Outfitted with sophisticated weaponry and monitoring equipment, they're able to engage a powerful adversary on the premier heavy combat force-on-force battleground in

24
00:02:16,583 --> 00:02:20,720
the world, transforming essentially from soldiers to combat-ready units.

25
00:02:20,721 --> 00:02:31,700
For a grueling 28 days, they will practice war, day and night, under war zone conditions on their own country's shore, to prepare themselves ultimately to defend it.

26
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:39,600
Combat readiness training in the armed forces is being stressed now more than ever before, and with good reason.

27
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:47,540
A lot of people say there are no threats to the United States right now, but I think we've proven that to be wrong over the last 10 years,

28
00:02:47,541 --> 00:02:52,080
where we've had more deployment of our forces than we had in the previous 30.

29
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:59,020
Established in 1980, the NTC has evolved rapidly from a dirt to a digital training ground.

30
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:05,300
The center's renowned Star Wars building features the military's most advanced computer and satellite technology

31
00:03:05,301 --> 00:03:11,080
to replicate and record actual combat maneuvers during force-on-force action on the battlefield.

32
00:03:11,081 --> 00:03:18,580
The original vision of the National Training Center was for a facility that could instrument

33
00:03:18,581 --> 00:03:21,620
and therefore track what was happening on the battlefield

34
00:03:21,621 --> 00:03:26,320
and provide a capability to feed back that information to units

35
00:03:26,321 --> 00:03:28,440
so they could leave here better than they came.

36
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:32,860
This instrumentation is made possible by the highly sophisticated

37
00:03:32,861 --> 00:03:36,840
Multiple Integrated Laser Engagement System, or MILES.

38
00:03:36,841 --> 00:03:42,840
Miles is basically a very elaborate laser tag system.

39
00:03:42,841 --> 00:03:49,680
We use it to replicate the effects of weaponry without having to actually shoot real bullets at each other.

40
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:56,840
It gives us the opportunity to fire a weapon, whether it be an individual soldier's weapon or a tank or a Bradley vehicle,

41
00:03:57,740 --> 00:04:01,260
and replicate the type of kill that would produce on the battlefield.

42
00:04:01,261 --> 00:04:11,260
The Miles system made us the crown jewel of any Army's training center.

43
00:04:11,261 --> 00:04:18,260
It allows soldiers to really see what they do right and wrong, and it's what makes this place the premier training center in the world.

44
00:04:18,261 --> 00:04:26,260
This is building 988, affectionately known as the Star Wars building, and we are currently standing in the operations center of this building,

45
00:04:26,261 --> 00:04:30,620
where we gather all the information during a typical rotation,

46
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:33,200
and then we feed this information back to the commander

47
00:04:33,201 --> 00:04:35,680
so that he can better his unit for future endeavors.

48
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:37,740
We are big brother.

49
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:39,580
We see all, know all in this building,

50
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:42,600
but the commander is not aware of what is taking place.

51
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:45,320
We are the people behind the scenes,

52
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:48,440
the Oz behind the curtain in Wizard of Oz, so to speak,

53
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:50,660
and they don't know that we exist,

54
00:04:50,661 --> 00:04:52,780
with the exception of that observer controller

55
00:04:52,781 --> 00:04:56,000
who is behind their units out there on the field,

56
00:04:56,001 --> 00:04:58,700
in his Humvee observing what is going on.

57
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:03,080
Unlike the Cowboys and Indians games that we used to play as children,

58
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:08,260
we've taken those factors out and made it more realistic towards warlike conditions here.

59
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:13,740
What we've done with our laser system or models of equipment that we have on each vehicle

60
00:05:13,741 --> 00:05:19,600
is as the laser emits from a transmitter and hits another object,

61
00:05:19,900 --> 00:05:22,280
whether it's a vehicle or a soldier,

62
00:05:22,281 --> 00:05:28,040
that laser has a special code in it that tells that individual or vehicle what is shooting at it.

63
00:05:29,160 --> 00:05:32,740
That information is then fed through the instrumentation system

64
00:05:32,741 --> 00:05:35,060
and brought back here to our computers,

65
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:38,800
and we know exactly what shot that vehicle, who shot that vehicle,

66
00:05:39,160 --> 00:05:42,240
and what levels of catastrophic kill that vehicle has become

67
00:05:42,241 --> 00:05:44,440
based upon the engagement that occurred.

68
00:05:45,920 --> 00:05:48,880
Because the brigade will have an actual enemy to fight against,

69
00:05:49,460 --> 00:05:52,540
Blank ammunition is used to simulate actual combat conditions.

70
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:56,300
The soldier's personal weapons fitted with the miles' equipment

71
00:05:56,301 --> 00:05:59,340
have to be fired with blank rounds to activate the laser.

72
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:02,520
For the Bradley fighting vehicle and Abrams' tank,

73
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:06,400
in place of blank firing rounds, special signature pieces are mounted.

74
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:09,180
When the Bradley gunner squeezes the trigger,

75
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:12,800
a spotlight on the barrel imitates muzzle flash as the laser fires.

76
00:06:13,540 --> 00:06:15,920
The Bradley can also emit a flash powder effect.

77
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:21,360
The tank has what's called a Hoffman device that will actually ignite a quarter stick of dynamite.

78
00:06:22,580 --> 00:06:25,420
A yellow light is mounted on the vehicles to indicate a hit or a kill.

79
00:06:26,580 --> 00:06:28,720
Four flashes and you're permanently out of action.

80
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:38,560
When the soldier is fired upon, the miles harness that he wears or the belts that the vehicles are equipped with

81
00:06:38,561 --> 00:06:41,620
have little sensors on that receive the laser beam.

82
00:06:41,621 --> 00:06:49,200
If the soldier becomes a casualty, then the buzzer that is located on that harness gives off a continuous sound.

83
00:06:49,540 --> 00:06:52,340
If he's a near miss, then it's a sporadic beep.

84
00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:59,880
It's a great system, and they've set it up so it's as realistic as possible and as close to real bullets as we can.

85
00:07:00,420 --> 00:07:04,960
But the laser doesn't hurt anybody, and everybody at the end comes back to life.

86
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:10,800
It is sort of like a Nintendo game where you have 3D graphics that you can follow along,

87
00:07:10,801 --> 00:07:15,200
And our ability to do so is based upon the data communications integrator,

88
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:18,900
which works off a cellular phone technology that is fitted on every vehicle

89
00:07:18,901 --> 00:07:21,520
and at least one man in a nine-man squad.

90
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:24,940
And what this device does, it emits a continuous signal

91
00:07:24,941 --> 00:07:29,160
to one of two receiver transmitter towers that we have located on the mountain.

92
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:33,760
Once those towers pick up that signal, it is then turned digitally back here to the building.

93
00:07:33,761 --> 00:07:38,440
So as the vehicle or the soldier moves or maneuvers out there in the desert,

94
00:07:38,441 --> 00:07:41,700
those movements are then monitored here on our computer systems.

95
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:47,600
The operations computer shows real-time activity as it happens on the battlefield.

96
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:50,020
The blue icons are friendly forces.

97
00:07:50,620 --> 00:07:51,540
The red are the enemy.

98
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:57,680
The dark blue lines indicate control measures that keep the fighting units within certain preset boundaries.

99
00:07:59,720 --> 00:08:06,220
The fire support element screen allows the operations center controller to see all the incoming artillery fires and mortar fires.

100
00:08:06,221 --> 00:08:10,040
as they go into the grid coordinates that were called in by the units.

101
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:14,740
The white box is our FM radio,

102
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:18,660
and each one of those squares on that white box represents a frequency.

103
00:08:19,180 --> 00:08:21,460
The operator who sits here at that desk,

104
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:23,560
all he has to do is press one of those squares,

105
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:26,540
and he can monitor any frequency of any unit that is out here training.

106
00:08:26,900 --> 00:08:32,580
Now, that mode of communication is used by the player unit who is out here.

107
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:36,160
That is the mode in which they send us all their situation reports.

108
00:08:36,560 --> 00:08:41,100
If you'll notice to the right of that, we have what we call the Observer Controller Control Station,

109
00:08:41,980 --> 00:08:44,980
and that is what the observer controllers in the field talk to us by.

110
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:48,920
So when we're getting a report from the unit by FM communication,

111
00:08:49,340 --> 00:08:54,760
the OC radio or Observer Controller radio that the OC is talking on is telling us what the real report is.

112
00:08:54,761 --> 00:09:01,580
And what we're able to do is compare both reports to see how well the brigade commander and his staff is tracking the battle.

113
00:09:01,581 --> 00:09:13,468
We can monitor the battle as it unfolds and take notes on certain critical areas that we believe that the unit might need to take a look at to better themselves for

114
00:09:13,469 --> 00:09:14,700
future endeavors.

115
00:09:16,300 --> 00:09:20,920
A civilian integrator monitors the AAR, or After Action Review Screen.

116
00:09:21,740 --> 00:09:29,520
As the battle develops, he captures significant moments and then produces still photos and graphics to create review materials of what took place.

117
00:09:30,420 --> 00:09:33,260
After the battle, these materials are provided to the field commander

118
00:09:33,261 --> 00:09:37,320
to let him see what the ground truth was, how the battle actually went,

119
00:09:37,840 --> 00:09:38,980
what really took place.

120
00:09:40,060 --> 00:09:41,680
There are no winners or losers.

121
00:09:42,180 --> 00:09:44,960
The whole intent of the National Training Center

122
00:09:44,961 --> 00:09:48,540
is to create warlike conditions for the unit commander

123
00:09:48,541 --> 00:09:53,540
so that he can learn his lessons and change his training philosophies

124
00:09:53,541 --> 00:09:55,660
prior to going to an actual combat situation.

125
00:09:55,661 --> 00:09:59,240
It has been common by soldiers here who were stationed at Desert Storm

126
00:09:59,241 --> 00:10:02,440
and have come out here for training, that the training out here was much tougher

127
00:10:02,441 --> 00:10:04,340
than what they experienced in Desert Storm.

128
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:09,500
The elaborate wargaming scenarios developed by the NTC's command

129
00:10:09,501 --> 00:10:11,340
are complex and totally realistic,

130
00:10:11,720 --> 00:10:16,840
all designed to expose and exploit the weaknesses of the visiting armored and mechanized brigades.

131
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:20,400
The unit now in preparation for the upcoming exercises

132
00:10:20,401 --> 00:10:22,855
will be a brigade combat team from one of the U.S.

133
00:10:22,856 --> 00:10:24,720
Army's most honored cavalry divisions.

134
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:35,440
Fort Hood, Texas, the biggest military installation in the free world,

135
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,140
and home base to the U.S. Army's largest operational formations.

136
00:10:40,420 --> 00:10:44,120
Approximately 36% of the country's heavy combat power is assembled here.

137
00:10:46,320 --> 00:10:50,660
As much as four months in advance of Colonel Kevin Wendell's brigade's scheduled rotation,

138
00:10:50,960 --> 00:10:55,660
the commanding general of the 1st Cav has already developed the actual force-on-force training scenarios

139
00:10:56,280 --> 00:10:58,460
with the operational command group at NTC.

140
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:06,860
We develop a list of tasks at every level that provides the focus for our training.

141
00:11:06,980 --> 00:11:12,480
Those are the tasks that we have to do to standard all year long.

142
00:11:13,260 --> 00:11:17,500
We tactically tailor forces specifically to meet the needs.

143
00:11:18,360 --> 00:11:24,380
So, for example, with this NTC rotation, not only do we have the heavy force component,

144
00:11:24,381 --> 00:11:30,060
We're also bringing a light infantry battalion from Fort Hill, New York, and a lift aviation battalion.

145
00:11:31,420 --> 00:11:35,420
The 1st Cavalry Division alone is comprised of 17,000 soldiers,

146
00:11:36,100 --> 00:11:42,160
and Colonel Wendell's 5,000-man brigade combat team is now in final preparation for their four-week deployment to Fort Irwin.

147
00:11:42,680 --> 00:11:46,360
Their ultimate goal is that by the end of their wargaming at the National Training Center,

148
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:50,000
they'll be transformed into what is known as the Division Ready Brigade,

149
00:11:50,001 --> 00:11:54,500
first in line for immediate deployment to a combat zone anywhere in the world.

150
00:11:56,560 --> 00:12:00,240
The 11th Armored Cavalry fights as the opposing force at the NTC

151
00:12:00,241 --> 00:12:03,040
in over 50 engagements during 10 rotations a year.

152
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:06,240
That's over 100 days of fighting in the Mojave Desert.

153
00:12:07,160 --> 00:12:09,100
War Games will return on Modern Marvels.

154
00:12:11,720 --> 00:12:13,740
We now return to War Games.

155
00:12:13,741 --> 00:12:21,420
As Colonel Wendell's brigade begins loading its armored weapons and support vehicles,

156
00:12:22,260 --> 00:12:24,580
all its personnel must also be prepared for departure

157
00:12:24,581 --> 00:12:28,480
exactly the same as if they were being summoned to an actual trouble spot anywhere in the world.

158
00:12:29,480 --> 00:12:31,360
Powers of attorney are signed for soldier spouses.

159
00:12:32,300 --> 00:12:34,040
All shots and inoculations are completed.

160
00:12:34,660 --> 00:12:37,120
Everything is in order for each soldier's personal file

161
00:12:37,121 --> 00:12:40,400
to assure his own readiness, his soldier readiness.

162
00:12:40,401 --> 00:12:47,940
For the soldiers in the first cab, there's a lot at stake, and they take pride in being members of this storied division.

163
00:12:48,540 --> 00:12:53,440
A division composed of cavalry units that have served America as far back as 1855,

164
00:12:54,440 --> 00:12:59,960
receiving their baptism under fire on the western plains and territories against Indians and Mexican soldiers,

165
00:13:00,760 --> 00:13:04,380
often living and dying by the sword in remote, isolated skirmishes.

166
00:13:04,381 --> 00:13:13,280
from 1921 to 1943 while stationed in Fort Bliss Texas they would see their traditional role as

167
00:13:13,281 --> 00:13:19,880
horse-mounted cavalry become less and less a part of the army's primary forces as World War II began

168
00:13:19,881 --> 00:13:26,400
to rage across the globe they realized they had become obsolete they desperately wanted to go into

169
00:13:26,401 --> 00:13:31,740
the Second World War finally the decision was made that they could go to serve in the Pacific

170
00:13:31,741 --> 00:13:32,514
theater.

171
00:13:32,515 --> 00:13:40,640
In 1943, under a series of ceremonies, the cavalry turned in their horses and picked

172
00:13:40,641 --> 00:13:42,981
up the rifle and became infantry.

173
00:13:42,982 --> 00:13:46,740
It wasn't long before the first cab became one of the

174
00:13:46,741 --> 00:13:49,345
Army's most entrusted rapid deployment forces.

175
00:13:49,346 --> 00:13:51,780
Their first major operation was the Adminty

176
00:13:51,781 --> 00:13:54,888
Islands, MacArthur's island hopping campaigns.

177
00:13:54,889 --> 00:13:57,860
The next operation was the liberation of the

178
00:13:57,861 --> 00:14:04,498
Philippines. This is where the division gets its nickname, First Team.

179
00:14:04,499 --> 00:14:05,920
The 1st Cavalry

180
00:14:05,921 --> 00:14:11,640
Division has a very proud history. We were the first in Manila. We were the first in

181
00:14:11,641 --> 00:14:17,550
Tokyo. It was in the streets of Tokyo that a proud tradition was honored.

182
00:14:17,551 --> 00:14:18,440
The Cavalry

183
00:14:18,441 --> 00:14:20,675
Officer's sword is his badge of honor.

184
00:14:20,676 --> 00:14:23,440
It's a directional staff as well as a defensive

185
00:14:23,441 --> 00:14:24,400
and offensive weapon.

186
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:27,200
The cavalry officer had lost his sword

187
00:14:27,201 --> 00:14:28,320
many, many years ago.

188
00:14:30,080 --> 00:14:31,820
Now, as the victorious

189
00:14:31,821 --> 00:14:33,580
first Cav soldiers marched in,

190
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:35,720
they carried samurai swords.

191
00:14:38,260 --> 00:14:39,600
In 1965,

192
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:41,560
the venerable 1st Cavalry Division

193
00:14:41,561 --> 00:14:43,000
became an air mobility unit

194
00:14:43,001 --> 00:14:45,660
and continued to live up to its first-team traditions

195
00:14:45,661 --> 00:14:46,700
in the Vietnam War.

196
00:14:49,320 --> 00:14:51,740
More recently, they've served with distinction

197
00:14:51,741 --> 00:14:53,220
in the Persian Gulf and the Balkans

198
00:14:53,221 --> 00:14:54,440
as a heavy armored division.

199
00:14:57,320 --> 00:15:00,000
We're there to be a heavy punch.

200
00:15:00,340 --> 00:15:02,660
We bring to the battlefield an awesome power

201
00:15:02,661 --> 00:15:04,020
with the Abrams tank,

202
00:15:04,740 --> 00:15:06,560
the Bradley infantry vehicle,

203
00:15:07,620 --> 00:15:10,400
the artillery systems, the aviation systems,

204
00:15:10,640 --> 00:15:13,560
the complete combined arms package that we have.

205
00:15:16,340 --> 00:15:18,600
We are a force to be reckoned with.

206
00:15:20,700 --> 00:15:23,060
It is a force that thrives on pressure

207
00:15:23,061 --> 00:15:28,260
knowing from experience that it has to be ready to pick up and go virtually anywhere at a moment's notice.

208
00:15:28,840 --> 00:15:34,620
In the 21st century, most scenarios where the military would be committed

209
00:15:34,621 --> 00:15:37,780
will happen much faster than they happened in the past.

210
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:44,320
So the responsiveness of the Army to get to a theater of operations anywhere in the world is paramount.

211
00:15:47,440 --> 00:15:52,480
As America's first team departs from Fort Hood for their next battle on the proving grounds of the NTC,

212
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:56,300
They're not told any specifics regarding their pending mission.

213
00:15:58,300 --> 00:16:01,000
All they know is that they can expect a hostile environment

214
00:16:01,001 --> 00:16:04,880
and formidable resistance against an equally skilled and seasoned force

215
00:16:04,881 --> 00:16:06,900
waiting for them on the battlefield.

216
00:16:10,900 --> 00:16:14,340
The resident opposing force, or OP-4 at Fort Irwin,

217
00:16:14,460 --> 00:16:17,840
consists of elements of the distinguished 11th Armored Cavalry Regiment.

218
00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:20,580
Like their counterparts in the 1st Cav Division,

219
00:16:20,581 --> 00:16:23,480
the 11th ACR has a long and impressive lineage,

220
00:16:24,140 --> 00:16:26,640
celebrating their 100th anniversary in 2001.

221
00:16:28,500 --> 00:16:33,120
In 1916, the 11th Cavalry was ordered to find the Mexican renegade Pancho Villa,

222
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:37,120
who had killed inhabitants of towns on both sides of the U.S.-Mexico border.

223
00:16:38,120 --> 00:16:41,520
On the 5th of May, they successfully attacked one of Villa's strongholds

224
00:16:41,521 --> 00:16:45,120
in what would become the Army's last full, proper mounted charge in U.S.

225
00:16:45,121 --> 00:16:45,920
Cavalry history.

226
00:16:45,921 --> 00:16:48,780
but by the onset of World War II

227
00:16:48,781 --> 00:16:50,780
the 11th was no longer horse-mounted cavalry

228
00:16:50,781 --> 00:16:53,120
and was sent overseas as an armored division

229
00:16:53,121 --> 00:16:55,260
fighting with the distinction of the Battle of the Bulge

230
00:16:55,261 --> 00:16:57,000
some 20 years later

