00:19:15,921 --> 00:19:21,880
it's been said that only the best are assigned to the 11th cab opposition force

278
00:19:21,881 --> 00:19:26,260
because the army wants the toughest individuals to fight against all rotation units

279
00:19:26,261 --> 00:19:32,020
most military officers and intelligence analysts acknowledge

280
00:19:32,021 --> 00:19:33,920
that in isolated combat

281
00:19:33,921 --> 00:19:37,260
an army with the world's most lethal and sophisticated arsenal

282
00:19:37,261 --> 00:19:40,100
will inevitably succumb to a better trained force,

283
00:19:40,800 --> 00:19:42,980
even one that possesses inferior weaponry.

284
00:19:44,400 --> 00:19:49,100
They embrace the philosophy of countless military minds throughout history who believed

285
00:19:49,101 --> 00:19:53,320
that to excel at war, they must first practice it.

286
00:19:58,240 --> 00:20:01,620
Each soldier's multiple integrated laser engagement system,

287
00:20:01,720 --> 00:20:05,200
or MILES, helmet, halo, and vest, costs over $1,500.

288
00:20:05,201 --> 00:20:09,200
War Games will return on Modern Marvels.

289
00:20:13,700 --> 00:20:17,480
While the tools involved in military war gaming have changed drastically,

290
00:20:18,060 --> 00:20:19,540
particularly in the last decade,

291
00:20:20,040 --> 00:20:23,940
the actual concept as a method of training has existed for thousands of years.

292
00:20:24,860 --> 00:20:26,780
The first recorded one is the Romans.

293
00:20:27,260 --> 00:20:30,420
It was recorded by a number of ancient historians about the Romans

294
00:20:30,421 --> 00:20:31,760
and their prowess on the battlefield,

295
00:20:31,761 --> 00:20:34,080
and of course that was all a result of training.

296
00:20:35,200 --> 00:20:42,940
Alexander the Great, Genghis Khan, England's Duke of Wellington, all rank among history's most successful commanders.

297
00:20:43,940 --> 00:20:50,300
Each indoctrinated his armies in deadly force-on-force drills and maneuvers as a way to build character, endurance, and cohesion.

298
00:20:51,660 --> 00:20:57,480
In colonial America, military training and tactics, like most everything else at the time, was imported from Europe.

299
00:20:58,280 --> 00:21:01,020
It was a Prussian, Baron Friedrich von Steuben,

300
00:21:01,440 --> 00:21:04,360
who was among the first to stress the importance of regular drills

301
00:21:04,361 --> 00:21:06,840
for the colony's embryonic army and local militias.

302
00:21:07,700 --> 00:21:10,520
As he was ascending to the rank of general in the Revolutionary War,

303
00:21:11,160 --> 00:21:11,860
he wrote,

304
00:21:12,180 --> 00:21:15,460
Regulations for the Order and Discipline of the Troops of the United States.

305
00:21:16,200 --> 00:21:18,740
The manual was widely used throughout the conflict

306
00:21:18,741 --> 00:21:23,440
and was so highly regarded that it served as America's official military handbook until 1812.

307
00:21:23,441 --> 00:21:28,940
However, with the development of its burgeoning navy,

308
00:21:29,420 --> 00:21:30,120
the U.S.

309
00:21:30,121 --> 00:21:33,700
government was soon capable of responding to international threats vital to its interests,

310
00:21:33,800 --> 00:21:35,420
and its military scope expanded.

311
00:21:36,300 --> 00:21:41,140
Yet too often, America's forces were spread too thin, with inadequate supplies and reinforcements.

312
00:21:42,040 --> 00:21:47,340
For many years after the Civil War, our armed forces had little or no opportunity to train together,

313
00:21:47,840 --> 00:21:49,280
either at home or overseas.

314
00:21:49,281 --> 00:21:57,220
During most of the late 19th and early 20th centuries, the United States Army was spread among numerous posts throughout the frontier.

315
00:21:57,820 --> 00:22:00,480
It was very difficult to get units together for any kind of war game.

316
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:06,960
Though some actions were taken to improve America's military preparedness and increase its reserves,

317
00:22:07,960 --> 00:22:10,820
the Great Depression halted military expansion in its tracks.

318
00:22:11,620 --> 00:22:14,800
Funding was cut, and morale across the military sagged.

319
00:22:14,801 --> 00:22:19,600
By 1933, U.S. Chief of Staff General Douglas MacArthur

320
00:22:19,601 --> 00:22:22,760
decried the Army's tanks as entirely useless for engagement

321
00:22:22,761 --> 00:22:24,480
against modern units on the battlefield.

322
00:22:25,700 --> 00:22:27,880
Then, gradually, over the next several years,

323
00:22:28,680 --> 00:22:30,420
Army appropriations began to rise,

324
00:22:30,540 --> 00:22:34,020
mostly in response to Germany's massive military buildup across the Atlantic.

325
00:22:35,160 --> 00:22:39,460
By the late 1930s, the U.S. Army commenced full-scale wargaming exercises

326
00:22:39,461 --> 00:22:42,400
in what was known as the Louisiana and Carolina Maneuvers.

327
00:22:42,401 --> 00:22:45,760
Louisiana and the Carolina menu

328
00:22:45,761 --> 00:22:48,480
was really the first time that we had an army-to-army training.

329
00:22:49,820 --> 00:22:53,800
We had core-to-core and smaller unit operations before then,

330
00:22:54,040 --> 00:22:57,340
but this is an opportunity for large army units to participate together,

331
00:22:57,780 --> 00:22:59,660
and also the use of combat arms

332
00:22:59,661 --> 00:23:01,280
where you're using them in different branches,

333
00:23:01,580 --> 00:23:03,380
cavalry, armor, and aviation.

334
00:23:06,680 --> 00:23:07,940
We were wargaming.

335
00:23:08,120 --> 00:23:10,640
We were preparing ourselves for the next war.

336
00:23:11,320 --> 00:23:16,900
And through that process, we were identifying where were our strengths, where were our weaknesses.

337
00:23:18,160 --> 00:23:22,840
It was also an opportunity to test some of our new equipment and also test new tactics.

338
00:23:23,300 --> 00:23:28,060
The use of the tank, although we had tanks in the First World War, we didn't use them very successfully.

339
00:23:28,180 --> 00:23:32,080
During the Louisiana-Carolina maneuvers, tanks were used significantly.

340
00:23:34,180 --> 00:23:39,540
Some of the key participants involved in these maneuvers went on to achieve success for America in World War II.

341
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:45,860
Men like Omar Bradley, George Patton, and Dwight Eisenhower.

342
00:23:50,480 --> 00:23:53,520
With the onset of the Cold War in the late 1940s,

343
00:23:54,060 --> 00:23:57,860
the U.S. Army resumed large-scale maneuvers at military bases throughout Europe.

344
00:23:59,200 --> 00:24:04,300
However, the problem with these exercises was that there was no way to judge them accurately and objectively.

345
00:24:04,301 --> 00:24:11,300
Back in the mid-1970s, we used to do exercises called reforgers in Germany.

346
00:24:11,301 --> 00:24:17,300
These were the return of forces to Germany to help the NATO countries.

347
00:24:17,301 --> 00:24:22,300
The units that were stationed there would have these huge maneuver exercises.

348
00:24:22,301 --> 00:24:27,300
You would go out on your tank and you would engage other forces and they would have big panels on their tank.

349
00:24:27,301 --> 00:24:33,300
You would say to your umpire, I shot orange panel number 83.

350
00:24:33,301 --> 00:24:39,260
And the guy in orange panel number 83 would say, no, I shot blue panel number 14 first.

351
00:24:41,800 --> 00:24:47,720
Even communication and integration between various branches of the U.S.'s own forces was at times flawed.

352
00:24:48,500 --> 00:24:51,800
By the early 1980s, the Pentagon had taken a renewed interest in wargaming,

353
00:24:52,260 --> 00:24:55,400
and the role of training institutions like the NTC was expanded.

354
00:24:57,000 --> 00:25:01,200
We'll receive an operations order from our higher headquarters once we arrive at National Training Center.

355
00:25:01,201 --> 00:25:08,600
As we develop the mission and decide how we want to approach it, we'll select a course of action.

356
00:25:10,160 --> 00:25:12,420
Embedded in that system is this wargaming.

357
00:25:14,420 --> 00:25:19,920
What you develop is an ability to see the fight, hopefully the way you think you're going to fight it.

358
00:25:20,660 --> 00:25:26,060
And it helps you look for those unexpected and other things out there you may have not planned for.

359
00:25:26,061 --> 00:25:32,760
Some of these unexpected elements range from nuclear to biological to chemical weapons.

360
00:25:33,680 --> 00:25:40,800
It's in circumstances like these that a unit's ability to assess the severity and extent of such an attack with immediate analysis and containment,

361
00:25:41,620 --> 00:25:44,580
and then to contain and control the damage to both men and machines,

362
00:25:45,260 --> 00:25:48,800
that can make the difference between success or failure of its assigned tasks.

363
00:25:48,801 --> 00:25:53,420
success or failure in a real wartime conflict

364
00:25:53,421 --> 00:25:55,660
will ultimately be measured in human lives

365
00:25:55,661 --> 00:25:57,220
lost or saved

366
00:25:57,221 --> 00:26:01,020
for this reason

367
00:26:01,021 --> 00:26:04,060
providing a variety of war zone pressure packed scenarios

368
00:26:04,061 --> 00:26:06,780
is high priority at the NTC's proving ground

369
00:26:06,781 --> 00:26:09,760
as Colonel Wendell's brigade is about to find out

370
00:26:09,761 --> 00:26:17,720
a whole generation has gone through the NTC

371
00:26:18,620 --> 00:26:21,660
Officers who went through as lieutenants are now coming back as lieutenant colonels.

372
00:26:22,540 --> 00:26:26,900
Since its inception over 20 years ago, almost one million soldiers have been trained.

373
00:26:27,820 --> 00:26:28,940
War Games will return.

374
00:26:39,760 --> 00:26:47,540
In any military operation, the daunting and sometimes overwhelming problem of how to move an army and all of its equipment to the battle zone

375
00:26:47,541 --> 00:26:50,240
is often as important as the tactics.

376
00:26:54,500 --> 00:26:57,900
One of the benefits of deploying to the National Training Center

377
00:26:57,901 --> 00:27:03,000
is it gives us an opportunity to replicate a strategic operational deployment.

378
00:27:04,660 --> 00:27:08,780
This operation will involve the loading and transporting of thousands of troops

379
00:27:08,781 --> 00:27:13,220
and all the weapons and support equipment needed for an actual conflict to a battlefield

380
00:27:13,221 --> 00:27:20,800
and then coordinating them in what is known as RSOI, or Reception, Staging, Onward Movement, and Integration.

381
00:27:21,680 --> 00:27:25,560
The entire scenario of moving from Fort Hood to a contested area,

382
00:27:25,960 --> 00:27:30,780
with all the planning, preparations, and execution of a military operation, will be recreated.

