457
00:34:35,240 --> 00:34:42,360
Fort Irwin employs over 2,600 civilians, many of them retired officers who participate in a variety of ways during war gaming.

458
00:34:43,400 --> 00:34:45,120
They serve an important function.

459
00:34:45,121 --> 00:34:51,400
One of the things we designed as part of the building of this place

460
00:34:51,401 --> 00:34:54,780
was how we integrate not only the soldiers and the leaders

461
00:34:54,781 --> 00:34:56,480
that train the units out in the field,

462
00:34:56,680 --> 00:34:59,940
but also the civilians here on the post that help us.

463
00:35:01,040 --> 00:35:04,800
We have contract civilians who play civilians on the battlefield.

464
00:35:06,040 --> 00:35:09,160
And what the brigade does and how they treat these people

465
00:35:09,161 --> 00:35:11,040
as they come in daily contact with them

466
00:35:11,041 --> 00:35:15,200
makes them susceptible to recruitment by our terrorist cells,

467
00:35:15,520 --> 00:35:17,620
which are working for the opposing force.

468
00:35:18,660 --> 00:35:21,440
As the rotational unit gets into the groove,

469
00:35:21,660 --> 00:35:25,520
either in the deployment week or during the actual battlefield campaign,

470
00:35:26,380 --> 00:35:27,940
there are events occurring,

471
00:35:28,420 --> 00:35:31,960
and I've had my job likened to that of being a Hollywood director,

472
00:35:32,440 --> 00:35:37,180
in that as things occur, we manipulate the scenario a little bit

473
00:35:37,181 --> 00:35:39,460
to test the brigade a little bit more.

474
00:35:41,040 --> 00:35:46,760
The ultimate test against the NTC's opposing force will be nothing short of the real thing,

475
00:35:47,460 --> 00:35:51,120
an extreme indoctrination into the harsh demands of modern warfare.

476
00:35:59,940 --> 00:36:04,920
During World War II, Italian prisoners of war built Fort Irwin's Officers Club.

477
00:36:05,800 --> 00:36:07,640
Today it's used as a fast food outlet.

478
00:36:07,641 --> 00:36:11,640
War Games will return on Modern Marvels.

479
00:36:16,640 --> 00:36:18,640
The Desert, Day One.

480
00:36:19,960 --> 00:36:25,600
Colonel Wendell's brigade has begun 15 continuous days and nights of force-on-force engagements on the battleground.

481
00:36:26,040 --> 00:36:28,940
Sleeping and eating at their posts, when and where they can.

482
00:36:30,020 --> 00:36:32,980
Repairing, restoring, and resupplying equipment and personnel.

483
00:36:33,580 --> 00:36:36,300
All under fully simulated combat conditions.

484
00:36:37,640 --> 00:36:43,640
At 7 a.m., they began fighting an offensive mission against an Op-4 motorized rifle battalion.

485
00:36:44,840 --> 00:36:49,780
As the battle rages between Colonel Davis' Op-4 and Colonel Wendell's Brigade Combat Team,

486
00:36:50,520 --> 00:36:54,580
infantry and mechanized units on both sides suffer heavy simulated casualties.

487
00:36:55,260 --> 00:36:56,300
And it's all on the record.

488
00:36:57,160 --> 00:37:02,240
With the NTC state-of-the-art technology, the MILES system, combat camera teams,

489
00:37:02,241 --> 00:37:04,480
and global positioning satellite tracking,

490
00:37:04,481 --> 00:37:11,480
Every action and reaction of these soldiers and vehicles is being recorded, visually and orally, in real time.

491
00:37:11,481 --> 00:37:21,152
Just as the system is designed to do, the lasers emitting from the mild units on every combat vehicle and every individual soldier's weapon are transmitting their

492
00:37:21,153 --> 00:37:23,480
actions with each weapon's unique code.

493
00:37:23,481 --> 00:37:28,240
letting both the troops on the battlefield and the commanders inside the Star Wars Center

494
00:37:28,241 --> 00:37:33,880
know exactly what kind of weapon is being fired, who is firing, and who's being fired at.

495
00:37:35,280 --> 00:37:39,820
All of this information is being received and recorded via the Data Communications Integrator.

496
00:37:40,980 --> 00:37:46,500
All levels of kills or casualties to both vehicles and personnel are constantly assessed as engagements occur.

497
00:37:49,240 --> 00:37:53,000
Now, during full-scale force-on-force battle operations,

498
00:37:53,480 --> 00:37:57,120
The nerve center of Fort Irwin's building 988 is running at full speed.

499
00:37:57,680 --> 00:38:02,780
If any soldier or commander on either side is inclined to try to beat this system, they'll lose.

500
00:38:04,160 --> 00:38:07,060
In the early years, before the MILES system was developed,

501
00:38:07,680 --> 00:38:10,360
it was still difficult to determine who was cheating and who wasn't.

502
00:38:11,160 --> 00:38:14,620
Today, every soldier knows exactly what the system is capable of doing.

503
00:38:15,120 --> 00:38:16,920
The motto at the NTC is,

504
00:38:17,240 --> 00:38:19,360
If you do it, we're going to see it.

505
00:38:19,840 --> 00:38:21,120
There's nowhere to hide.

506
00:38:23,480 --> 00:38:31,880
This multitude of electronic imaging and information is allowing the command group to provide instant radio feedback to their observer controllers in the field to

507
00:38:31,881 --> 00:38:34,680
evaluate and optimize a particular unit's performance.

508
00:38:37,360 --> 00:38:44,480
During the force-on-force exercises, these highly skilled observer controllers constantly cover all areas of the battleground.

509
00:38:45,620 --> 00:38:47,760
They're regarded as the best of the best.

510
00:38:48,580 --> 00:38:51,720
NTC veterans who help mediate and instruct during the conflict.

511
00:38:52,520 --> 00:38:55,420
They're also responsible for simulating weapons effects.

512
00:38:57,880 --> 00:39:02,360
We have the ability to monitor both where they're doing well or where they're deficient.

513
00:39:02,460 --> 00:39:06,160
So we can anticipate where the problems are going to be once the battle kicks off

514
00:39:06,161 --> 00:39:08,300
because of what the unit did or didn't do.

515
00:39:09,200 --> 00:39:12,940
If I'm doing my job right and my battalion's doing what we're supposed to do,

516
00:39:13,440 --> 00:39:17,500
it's worse setting conditions for the success of the brigade.

517
00:39:21,720 --> 00:39:25,520
Long days of grueling force-on-force laser battles continue into night fighting.

518
00:39:26,600 --> 00:39:31,040
And as the Brigade's last experience at the NTC, live fire is introduced.

519
00:39:36,660 --> 00:39:38,800
Working amid the modern weapons of war,

520
00:39:39,260 --> 00:39:44,200
the ability to master the power, accuracy, and maneuverability of their most lethal weaponry

521
00:39:44,201 --> 00:39:46,380
is essential to the Brigade's success.

522
00:39:51,720 --> 00:39:59,980
At day's end, however, it's neither the number of kills nor the amount of damage inflicted

523
00:39:59,981 --> 00:40:03,980
that determines whether a brigade's training objectives have really been achieved.

524
00:40:04,840 --> 00:40:08,980
This determination is left solely up to the brain trust in the operations group.

525
00:40:09,820 --> 00:40:11,940
Usually, within six hours of an action,

526
00:40:12,780 --> 00:40:16,680
the brigade's commander and his team will sit down with members of the NTC Operations Command

527
00:40:16,681 --> 00:40:17,880
and discuss what's happened.

528
00:40:17,881 --> 00:40:23,560
A critical evaluation process known as the After Action Review, or AAR.

529
00:40:25,380 --> 00:40:31,000
The After Action Review is probably the most important part of what we do out here.

530
00:40:31,500 --> 00:40:33,980
We ask one question when we start AAR.

531
00:40:34,420 --> 00:40:36,020
Did you accomplish the mission?

532
00:40:36,800 --> 00:40:40,500
If the answer is yes, then we ask a series of other questions.

533
00:40:40,740 --> 00:40:42,000
How could we have done that better?

534
00:40:42,660 --> 00:40:46,780
How could we have done that differently and execute our mission with more efficiency and more effectiveness?

535
00:40:46,781 --> 00:40:52,360
So both sides on this battlefield are learning how to be better soldiers in America Army.

536
00:40:54,480 --> 00:40:58,220
The ultimate challenge is that the brigade combat team from Fort Hood

537
00:40:58,221 --> 00:41:01,400
will absorb as much from their finishers as from their successes.

538
00:41:02,540 --> 00:41:06,260
The brigades that have gone through the National Training Center

539
00:41:06,261 --> 00:41:09,180
many times leave here having to lick their wounds

540
00:41:09,181 --> 00:41:12,080
because they have fought against the world-class Op 4.

541
00:41:12,500 --> 00:41:14,500
They have fought the best.

542
00:41:14,501 --> 00:41:22,680
and by shedding our sweat here and dying with miles with your peeper going off,

543
00:41:22,980 --> 00:41:27,140
we're learning the lessons of war without having to spill blood.

544
00:41:29,100 --> 00:41:32,160
There will be multiple deaths at Fort Irwin.

545
00:41:32,460 --> 00:41:35,300
I've died out here at least six times as a player

546
00:41:35,301 --> 00:41:39,760
and what I tell you is every time I died, I learned a valuable lesson

547
00:41:39,761 --> 00:41:44,460
which I hope will help me survive if we ever come to a real combat situation.

548
00:41:44,500 --> 00:41:50,580
again. You learn from those kind of experiences like you would learn from no other.

549
00:41:51,860 --> 00:41:55,228
The NTC, very simply put, is the national treasure.

550
00:41:55,229 --> 00:41:57,540
Because we did not win Desert Storm

551
00:41:57,541 --> 00:42:05,980
in 100 hours, it took 15 years of tough, hard-nosed, no-slack-on-the-standards training.

552
00:42:08,440 --> 00:42:13,140
The training methods and technologies will continue to evolve at the NTC to meet the

553
00:42:13,141 --> 00:42:16,360
new challenges and conditions that arise for U.S. armed forces.

554
00:42:18,680 --> 00:42:23,440
In the changing political world, in the changing international sphere,

555
00:42:24,100 --> 00:42:26,840
in the changing technology that we're undergoing at this time,

556
00:42:26,940 --> 00:42:29,100
the National Training Center is changing itself as well.

557
00:42:29,920 --> 00:42:33,280
In order to remain relevant, in order to remain current,

558
00:42:33,281 --> 00:42:35,860
the National Training Center is evolving forward,

559
00:42:36,580 --> 00:42:38,280
not just fighting the battles of old,

560
00:42:38,520 --> 00:42:40,940
but preparing the Army to fight the battles of the future.

561
00:42:43,140 --> 00:42:47,340
While many soldiers refer to a rotation at the NTC as the Super Bowl of training,

562
00:42:48,400 --> 00:42:53,800
there is no glory, no riches, and no trips to Disneyland when this contest is over.

563
00:42:55,260 --> 00:43:00,240
But they have achieved an unmatched level of combat readiness, ready for anything.

564
00:43:02,000 --> 00:43:06,980
Today's Army is made up of professionals, men and women who take pride in their front-line capabilities

565
00:43:06,981 --> 00:43:09,560
and their role in the defense of their country.

566
00:43:09,561 --> 00:43:16,300
we are the only nation in the world that bows our service to a piece of paper

567
00:43:16,301 --> 00:43:24,920
a series of of ideas and ideals other nations will pledge to defend a king or a country land

568
00:43:24,921 --> 00:43:31,200
or a queen and we're the only ones that say we have some important ideas that we want to defend

569
00:43:31,201 --> 00:43:36,580
our soldiers i think you can ask them and they'll give you a they'll give you myriad reasons for

570
00:43:36,581 --> 00:43:37,835
join in the military.

571
00:43:37,836 --> 00:43:41,600
And the biggest piece of what they do is support and defend the

572
00:43:41,601 --> 00:43:43,829
Constitution of the United States.

573
00:43:43,830 --> 00:43:47,500
Hopefully none of them will ever be called to see combat

574
00:43:47,501 --> 00:43:51,074
and have shots fired in anger at them other than a laser beam.

575
00:43:51,075 --> 00:43:52,400
But if they do, this is

576
00:43:52,401 --> 00:43:53,780
the place that prepares them for it.

577
00:43:54,560 --> 00:44:00,320
In today's world, American citizens are often reminded that just as they might entrust their

578
00:44:00,321 --> 00:44:04,856
health to physicians, they entrust their collective future to these soldiers.

579
00:44:04,857 --> 00:44:05,740
And it's places

580
00:44:05,741 --> 00:44:07,120
like the National Training Center

581
00:44:07,121 --> 00:44:09,020
where these extraordinary young men and women

582
00:44:09,021 --> 00:44:11,440
learn how to put their lives on the line for them.

583
00:44:12,220 --> 00:44:13,700
Time and time again.

